Add a CameraSensorHelper for the SK Hynix HI-1337 as found on the Samsung
Galaxy Tab S9 Wi-Fi (gts9wifi), where the port registers the sensor as
"hi1337-gts9u".

Without a helper, libcamera's software ISP cannot create one:

    IPASoft: Failed to create camera sensor helper for hi1337-gts9u

which leaves AGC completely inert. The sensor then stays at whatever
analogue gain it was left at -- in practice the maximum (240, i.e. 16x) --
producing an extremely noisy image. The IPA's black-level correction is also
skipped, which shows up as a heavy green cast in the processed output.

The analogue gain register (0x0213, codes 0..240) encodes
gain = 1 + code / 16, i.e. 1x..16x -- the same scheme libcamera already uses
for the Hynix HM-1246 -- and the pedestal is 64 at 10 bits, i.e. 4096 scaled
to the 16-bit value blackLevel_ expects (the convention the IMX219 entry
uses).

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S9 Wi-Fi: with the helper, AGC actively drives the
gain instead of leaving it frozen, and the processed output is correctly
coloured (no green cast).

Apply with: patch -p1 < add-hi1337-gts9u-camera-sensor-helper.patch

--- a/src/ipa/libipa/camera_sensor_helper.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/libipa/camera_sensor_helper.cpp
@@ -530,6 +530,27 @@
 };
 REGISTER_CAMERA_SENSOR_HELPER("hm1246", CameraSensorHelperHm1246)
 
+class CameraSensorHelperHi1337 : public CameraSensorHelper
+{
+public:
+	CameraSensorHelperHi1337()
+	{
+		/*
+		 * SK Hynix HI-1337, a 10-bit sensor.  The analogue gain
+		 * register (0x0213, codes 0..240) encodes
+		 * gain = 1 + code / 16, i.e. 1x..16x, the same scheme as the
+		 * Hynix HM-1246.  The pedestal is 64 at 10 bits, i.e. 4096
+		 * scaled to a 16-bit pixel width as blackLevel_ requires.
+		 *
+		 * The gts9wifi port registers this part as "hi1337-gts9u",
+		 * which is the model string the helper factory matches on.
+		 */
+		gain_ = AnalogueGainLinear{ 1, 16, 0, 16 };
+		blackLevel_ = 4096;
+	}
+};
+REGISTER_CAMERA_SENSOR_HELPER("hi1337-gts9u", CameraSensorHelperHi1337)
+
 class CameraSensorHelperImx214 : public CameraSensorHelper
 {
